IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `cornt-dates`

-- Coroners Act 1993 (NT) -- the questions of the family of the person who died.
--
-- SEAT: the individual the Act is applied to when a death is reported: a member of the family of the person who died,
-- usually the senior next of kin, who may have to report the death, is told of decisions, may object to an autopsy or
-- exhumation, may go to the Supreme Court, may take part in an inquest (and may be called as a witness at it), and may
-- later want to use what the coroner found in a claim. "Am I the senior next of kin?", "Is this a reportable death?",
-- "Must I report it?", "Must there be an inquest?", "By when must I go to the Supreme Court?", "Can the autopsy go
-- ahead yet?", "Can I use the coroner's findings in my claim?". The coroner, the coroner's clerk, the police, the
-- Attorney-General and the Supreme Court get no seat: what they decide or believe is asked as a fact, and their duties
-- are encoded for what they mean to the family.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, a number, a date, a choice from a list). No question takes a DEONTIC or an
-- EVENT. The family's steps with fixed deadlines (applying to the Supreme Court within 14 days, s 16(2), or within
-- 48 hours, ss 21(3), 23(3), 24(4)) are one regulative rule run inside the questions.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: s 3 (senior next of kin; married, spouse), s 11(2) (record not evidence), s 12(1), (1A) and (2)
-- (reportable death; duty to report), s 13(1) (information), s 14 (duty to investigate), s 15 (inquests), s 16 (decision
-- not to hold an inquest; Supreme Court), s 17 (control of the body), s 21 (application for autopsy), s 22 (notice of a
-- custody autopsy), s 23 (objection to autopsy), s 24 (exhumation), s 37 (advertisement of inquest), s 38 (witness
-- certificates), s 40(2), (3) (rights at an inquest), s 43(1)(c), (2) (non-publication of the name), s 44 (new inquest),
-- s 45 (coroner not a witness), and the maximum penalties of the offences a member of the public can commit (ss 12(2),
-- 13(1), 19(4), 32(2), 33(4), 36(2), 41(3), 43(2), 46). Out, with reasons, in NOTES.md.
--
-- Edition: Coroners Act 1993 (NT), reprint REPC019 as in force at 28 November 2022 (the current reprint on
-- 6 November 2026), deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/Coroners-Act-1993-NT-current-reprint-REPC019.txt.
-- No regulations are deposited; the numbers used are all in the Act. Penalty units are given as units: their dollar
-- value is set under the Penalty Units Act 2009 (NT), not deposited.
--
-- Copyright in Northern Territory legislation is owned by the Northern Territory. Quoted from an unofficial copy under
-- the NT copyright policy of 8 October 1996 and the owner's ruling of 24 October 2026 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments the Act hands to the coroner or the Supreme Court are
-- INPUTS ("if the coroner believes ..."). Answers follow the rulings on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05
-- (NOTES.md): an answer that is the text's own no is marked "(the text's no)"; an answer where the text is limited by its
-- own wording or silent is marked "NOTHING-kind".

§ `Coroners Act 1993 (NT): the questions of the family of the person who died`

§§ `s 3 -- Senior next of kin`

-- s 3: "senior next of kin, in relation to a deceased person, means:
--   (a) where a person was, immediately before death, married - the person's spouse; or
--   (b) where the person was not, immediately before death, married or, if married, the spouse is not available - the
--       person's son or daughter of or over 18 years; or
--   (c) where a spouse, son or daughter is not available - the person's parent; or
--   (d) where a spouse, son, daughter or parent is not available - the person's brother or sister of or over 18 years; or
--   (e) where a person is an Aborigine - a person who, according to the customs and tradition of the community or group
--       to which the person belongs, is an appropriate person; or
--   (f) where paragraphs (a) to (e) do not apply or a person who would be the senior next of kin under those paragraphs
--       is not available - a person who immediately before the death of the deceased person had a relationship with the
--       deceased person that, in the opinion of the coroner, is sufficient for the purpose of being the senior next of
--       kin."
-- "married includes being in a de facto relationship"; "spouse includes a person's de facto partner".
-- Plain gloss: the one family member the coroner deals with, in this order: partner, adult child, parent, adult brother
-- or sister; for an Aboriginal person, also the appropriate person by custom; failing all of them, someone the coroner
-- thinks close enough. A "means" definition: when no paragraph gives the asker the place, the answer is the text's no.
-- Fork F1: paragraph (e) is read as standing beside (a) to (d), not in place of them. Fork F2: in (c) and (d), "son or
-- daughter" means one of or over 18 (the (b) qualification). "not available" and "Aborigine" are not defined: asked.
DECLARE `My relationship to the person who died` IS ONE OF
    `their spouse or de facto partner immediately before the death`
    `their son or daughter`
    `their parent`
    `their brother or sister`
    `someone else (another relative, a friend, a carer)`

DECLARE `Am I the senior next of kin` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you are the senior next of kin as the spouse or de facto partner (s 3, paragraph (a))`
    `yes: you are the senior next of kin as a son or daughter aged 18 or over (s 3, paragraph (b)); any other available adult son or daughter is so equally`
    `yes: you are the senior next of kin as a parent (s 3, paragraph (c)); the other parent, if available, is so equally`
    `yes: you are the senior next of kin as a brother or sister aged 18 or over (s 3, paragraph (d)); any other available adult brother or sister is so equally`
    `yes: you are the senior next of kin as the appropriate person under the customs and tradition of the community or group to which the person who died belonged (s 3, paragraph (e))`
    `yes: you are the senior next of kin as a person whose relationship with the person who died the coroner considers sufficient (s 3, paragraph (f))`
    `no: on these facts you are not the senior next of kin (s 3, a "means" definition: the text's no)`

@export Am I the senior next of kin of the person who died (Coroners Act 1993 (NT) s 3)? The senior next of kin is, in order, the spouse or de facto partner, an adult son or daughter, a parent, an adult brother or sister; for an Aboriginal person, also the appropriate person by custom; failing all of them, a person the coroner considers close enough. The senior next of kin is the one the coroner must tell of decisions, and who may object to an autopsy or exhumation.
GIVEN `my relationship` IS A `My relationship to the person who died` @desc How you were related to the person who died
      `my age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in whole years
      `a spouse is available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Apart from you, whether the person who died was married or in a de facto relationship immediately before death and that spouse or partner is available
      `an adult son or daughter is available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Apart from you, whether the person who died has a son or daughter aged 18 or over who is available
      `a parent is available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Apart from you, whether a parent of the person who died is available
      `an adult brother or sister is available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Apart from you, whether the person who died has a brother or sister aged 18 or over who is available
      `Aboriginal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who died was an Aboriginal person
      `I am the appropriate person by custom` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, under the customs and tradition of the community or group to which the person who died belonged, you are an appropriate person to be the senior next of kin
      `someone else is the appropriate person by custom` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Apart from you, whether someone who is an appropriate person under those customs and tradition is available
      `the coroner considers my relationship sufficient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had a relationship with the person immediately before the death that the coroner considers sufficient for you to be the senior next of kin
GIVETH A `Am I the senior next of kin`
`s 3 -- am I the senior next of kin` `my relationship` `my age` `a spouse is available` `an adult son or daughter is available` `a parent is available` `an adult brother or sister is available` `Aboriginal` `I am the appropriate person by custom` `someone else is the appropriate person by custom` `the coroner considers my relationship sufficient` MEANS
    IF `my relationship` EQUALS `their spouse or de facto partner immediately before the death`
    THEN `yes: you are the senior next of kin as the spouse or de facto partner (s 3, paragraph (a))`
    ELSE IF `my relationship` EQUALS `their son or daughter` AND `my age` AT LEAST 18 AND (NOT `a spouse is available`)
         THEN `yes: you are the senior next of kin as a son or daughter aged 18 or over (s 3, paragraph (b)); any other available adult son or daughter is so equally`
         ELSE IF `my relationship` EQUALS `their parent` AND (NOT `a spouse is available`) AND (NOT `an adult son or daughter is available`)
              THEN `yes: you are the senior next of kin as a parent (s 3, paragraph (c)); the other parent, if available, is so equally`
              ELSE IF `my relationship` EQUALS `their brother or sister` AND `my age` AT LEAST 18 AND (NOT `a spouse is available`) AND (NOT `an adult son or daughter is available`) AND (NOT `a parent is available`)
                   THEN `yes: you are the senior next of kin as a brother or sister aged 18 or over (s 3, paragraph (d)); any other available adult brother or sister is so equally`
                   ELSE IF `Aboriginal` AND `I am the appropriate person by custom`
                        THEN `yes: you are the senior next of kin as the appropriate person under the customs and tradition of the community or group to which the person who died belonged (s 3, paragraph (e))`
                        ELSE IF `nobody under (a) to (e) is available` AND `the coroner considers my relationship sufficient`
                             THEN `yes: you are the senior next of kin as a person whose relationship with the person who died the coroner considers sufficient (s 3, paragraph (f))`
                             ELSE `no: on these facts you are not the senior next of kin (s 3, a "means" definition: the text's no)`
    WHERE
        `nobody under (a) to (e) is available` MEANS
                (NOT `a spouse is available`)
            AND (NOT `an adult son or daughter is available`)
            AND (NOT `a parent is available`)
            AND (NOT `an adult brother or sister is available`)
            AND (NOT (`Aboriginal` AND `someone else is the appropriate person by custom`))

§§ `s 12 -- Reportable deaths`

-- s 12(1): "reportable death means:
--   (a) a death where: (i) the body of a deceased person is in the Territory; or (ii) the death occurred in the
--       Territory; or (iii) the cause of the death occurred in the Territory; being a death: (iv) that appears to have
--       been unexpected, unnatural or violent or to have resulted, directly or indirectly, from an accident or injury; or
--       (v) that occurred during an anaesthetic; or (vi) that occurred as a result of an anaesthetic and is not due to
--       natural causes; or (vii) of a person who, immediately before death, was a person held in care or custody; or
--       (viii) that was caused or contributed to by injuries sustained while the person was held in custody; or (ix) of a
--       person whose identity is unknown; or
--   (b) the death of a person who ordinarily resided in the Territory at the time of death that occurred at a place
--       outside the Territory where the cause of death is not certified by a person who, under a law in force in the
--       place, is a legally qualified medical practitioner."
-- A "means" definition: when neither paragraph is met, the answer is the text's no.
-- Plain gloss: a death the coroner must hear about: sudden, violent, accidental or anaesthetic deaths, deaths in care or
-- custody, and unidentified bodies, with a link to the Territory; or a Territory resident's uncertified death elsewhere.
DECLARE `Where the death happened` IS ONE OF
    `in the Territory: the body is in the Territory, or the death or its cause occurred there`
    `outside the Territory, and the person ordinarily lived in the Territory`
    `outside the Territory, and the person did not ordinarily live in the Territory`

DECLARE `What kind of death it was` IS ONE OF
    `it appears unexpected, unnatural or violent, or to have resulted directly or indirectly from an accident or injury`
    `it occurred during an anaesthetic`
    `it occurred as a result of an anaesthetic and is not due to natural causes`
    `none of these: an expected death from natural causes`

-- s 12(1) "person held in care means: (a) a child who is in the CEO's care as defined in the Care and Protection of
-- Children Act 2007; or (b) a patient who, under the Mental Health and Related Services Act 1998 is in custody whether in
-- a hospital or temporarily removed from a hospital." The other Acts' terms are asked.
DECLARE `Care immediately before death` IS ONE OF
    `not held in care`
    `a child in the CEO's care under the Care and Protection of Children Act 2007`
    `a patient in custody under the Mental Health and Related Services Act 1998, in a hospital or temporarily removed from one`

-- s 12(1) "person held in custody means: (a) a person in the custody or control of: (i) a police officer; or (ii) a member
-- of the Police Force of a State or another Territory of the Commonwealth or of the Australian Federal Police; or (iii)
-- the Commissioner of Correctional Services; or (iv) a sheriff appointed under the Sheriff Act 1962; or (b) a person
-- detained in: (i) a custodial correctional facility ...; or (ii) a detention centre approved under the Youth Justice
-- Act 2005; and includes a person in the process of being taken into or escaping from: (c) the custody or control of a
-- person mentioned in paragraph (a); or (d) detention in a place mentioned in paragraph (b)."
-- s 12(1A): "a person is also taken to be held in custody if he or she: (a) is detained anywhere in the Territory by a
-- person authorised to do so under any Act or law in force in the Territory, including a law of the Commonwealth; or
-- (b) is in the process of escaping from detention mentioned in paragraph (a)."
DECLARE `Custody immediately before death` IS ONE OF
    `not held in custody`
    `in the custody or control of a Territory police officer`
    `in the custody or control of a member of the police of a State or another Territory, or of the Australian Federal Police`
    `in the custody or control of the Commissioner of Correctional Services`
    `in the custody or control of a sheriff appointed under the Sheriff Act 1962`
    `detained in a custodial correctional facility (a prison)`
    `detained in a detention centre approved under the Youth Justice Act 2005`
    `being taken into, or escaping from, any of that custody, control or detention`
    `detained anywhere in the Territory by a person authorised to do so under any Act or law in force there, including Commonwealth law`
    `escaping from such detention in the Territory`

GIVEN `care` IS A `Care immediately before death`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `held in care` `care` IF NOT (`care` EQUALS `not held in care`)

GIVEN `custody` IS A `Custody immediately before death`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `held in custody` `custody` IF NOT (`custody` EQUALS `not held in custody`)

DECLARE `Is it a reportable death` IS ONE OF
    `yes: a reportable death under paragraph (a) of the definition in s 12(1)`
    `yes: a reportable death under paragraph (b) of the definition in s 12(1): a Territory resident who died outside the Territory, the cause of death not certified by a medical practitioner there`
    `no: not a reportable death (s 12(1), a "means" definition: the text's no)`

@export Is the death of my relative a "reportable death" under the Coroners Act 1993 (NT) s 12(1)? A reportable death is one with a link to the Territory that was unexpected, unnatural, violent, accidental, during or from an anaesthetic, of a person held in care or custody, from injuries in custody, or of an unidentified person; or the death outside the Territory of a Territory resident whose cause of death was not certified by a doctor there.
GIVEN `where it happened` IS A `Where the death happened` @desc Where the death happened, and whether the person ordinarily lived in the Northern Territory
      `what kind of death` IS A `What kind of death it was` @desc Which of these best describes the death
      `care` IS A `Care immediately before death` @desc Whether, immediately before death, the person was held in care, and how
      `custody` IS A `Custody immediately before death` @desc Whether, immediately before death, the person was held in custody, and how
      `injuries in custody` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death was caused or contributed to by injuries the person sustained while held in custody
      `identity unknown` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the identity of the person who died is unknown
      `certified` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a death outside the Territory: whether the cause of death was certified by a person who is a legally qualified medical practitioner under the law of that place
GIVETH A `Is it a reportable death`
`s 12 -- is it a reportable death` `where it happened` `what kind of death` `care` `custody` `injuries in custody` `identity unknown` `certified` MEANS
    IF `where it happened` EQUALS `in the Territory: the body is in the Territory, or the death or its cause occurred there` AND `a head of paragraph (a) is met`
    THEN `yes: a reportable death under paragraph (a) of the definition in s 12(1)`
    ELSE IF `where it happened` EQUALS `outside the Territory, and the person ordinarily lived in the Territory` AND (NOT `certified`)
         THEN `yes: a reportable death under paragraph (b) of the definition in s 12(1): a Territory resident who died outside the Territory, the cause of death not certified by a medical practitioner there`
         ELSE `no: not a reportable death (s 12(1), a "means" definition: the text's no)`
    WHERE
        `a head of paragraph (a) is met` MEANS
               (NOT (`what kind of death` EQUALS `none of these: an expected death from natural causes`))
            OR `held in care` `care`
            OR `held in custody` `custody`
            OR `injuries in custody`
            OR `identity unknown`

GIVEN `where it happened` IS A `Where the death happened`, `what kind of death` IS A `What kind of death it was`, `care` IS A `Care immediately before death`, `custody` IS A `Custody immediately before death`, `injuries in custody` IS A BOOLEAN, `identity unknown` IS A BOOLEAN, `certified` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a reportable death` `where it happened` `what kind of death` `care` `custody` `injuries in custody` `identity unknown` `certified` IF
    NOT (`s 12 -- is it a reportable death` `where it happened` `what kind of death` `care` `custody` `injuries in custody` `identity unknown` `certified`
            EQUALS `no: not a reportable death (s 12(1), a "means" definition: the text's no)`)

-- s 12(2): "A person who has reasonable grounds to believe that a reportable death has not been reported must report the
-- death as soon as possible to a coroner or police officer. Maximum penalty: 40 penalty units."
-- s 13(1): "A person who reports a death must give to the coroner investigating the death any information that may help
-- the investigation. Maximum penalty: 40 penalty units."
-- "As soon as possible" fixes no number, so the duty is not a regulative rule with a WITHIN (NOTES.md). Whether the
-- person has reasonable grounds is the person's own fact. Fork F3: when the person has no such grounds, the answer is
-- the text's no for s 12(2) (the duty is on "a person who has reasonable grounds").
DECLARE `Must I report the death` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you must report it as soon as possible to a coroner or a police officer (s 12(2); maximum penalty 40 penalty units), and then give the coroner any information that may help the investigation (s 13(1); maximum penalty 40 penalty units)`
    `no duty under s 12(2): you do not have reasonable grounds to believe that it has not been reported (the text's no)`
    `no duty under s 12(2): not a reportable death on these facts (the text's no)`

@export Must I report my relative's death to a coroner or the police (Coroners Act 1993 (NT) s 12(2))? A person who has reasonable grounds to believe that a reportable death has not been reported must report it as soon as possible to a coroner or a police officer, and must then give the coroner any information that may help (s 13(1)).
GIVEN `where it happened` IS A `Where the death happened` @desc Where the death happened, and whether the person ordinarily lived in the Northern Territory
      `what kind of death` IS A `What kind of death it was` @desc Which of these best describes the death
      `care` IS A `Care immediately before death` @desc Whether, immediately before death, the person was held in care, and how
      `custody` IS A `Custody immediately before death` @desc Whether, immediately before death, the person was held in custody, and how
      `injuries in custody` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death was caused or contributed to by injuries the person sustained while held in custody
      `identity unknown` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the identity of the person who died is unknown
      `certified` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a death outside the Territory: whether the cause of death was certified by a person who is a legally qualified medical practitioner under the law of that place
      `grounds to believe not reported` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have reasonable grounds to believe that the death has not been reported to a coroner or the police
GIVETH A `Must I report the death`
`s 12 -- must I report the death` `where it happened` `what kind of death` `care` `custody` `injuries in custody` `identity unknown` `certified` `grounds to believe not reported` MEANS
    IF NOT (`a reportable death` `where it happened` `what kind of death` `care` `custody` `injuries in custody` `identity unknown` `certified`)
    THEN `no duty under s 12(2): not a reportable death on these facts (the text's no)`
    ELSE IF `grounds to believe not reported`
         THEN `yes: you must report it as soon as possible to a coroner or a police officer (s 12(2); maximum penalty 40 penalty units), and then give the coroner any information that may help the investigation (s 13(1); maximum penalty 40 penalty units)`
         ELSE `no duty under s 12(2): you do not have reasonable grounds to believe that it has not been reported (the text's no)`

§§ `s 14 -- Investigation`

-- s 14(1): "A coroner has jurisdiction to investigate a death if it appears to the coroner that the death is or may be a
-- reportable death." (2): "A coroner to whom a death is reported must, if it appears to the coroner that the death is or
-- may be a reportable death, investigate it." (3): "A coroner need not investigate a death if an investigation or
-- inquest is held in a State or another Territory."
-- Fork F4: "it appears to the coroner" is answered from the facts: a death that is reportable on the facts is taken to
-- appear so to the coroner. A coroner may still take jurisdiction over a death that only "may be" reportable.
DECLARE `Must the coroner investigate` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the coroner to whom the death was reported must investigate it (s 14(2))`
    `the coroner need not investigate it, because an investigation or inquest is held in a State or another Territory (s 14(3)); the coroner still may`
    `not yet: s 14(2) binds the coroner to whom the death is reported, and it has not been reported; a person with reasonable grounds to believe it has not been reported must report it (s 12(2))`
    `not required by s 14(2): not a reportable death on these facts (the text's no); a coroner has jurisdiction only if it appears to the coroner that the death is or may be a reportable death (s 14(1))`

@export Must a coroner investigate my relative's death (Coroners Act 1993 (NT) s 14)? A coroner to whom a reportable death is reported must investigate it, but need not if an investigation or inquest is held in a State or another Territory.
GIVEN `where it happened` IS A `Where the death happened` @desc Where the death happened, and whether the person ordinarily lived in the Northern Territory
      `what kind of death` IS A `What kind of death it was` @desc Which of these best describes the death
      `care` IS A `Care immediately before death` @desc Whether, immediately before death, the person was held in care, and how
      `custody` IS A `Custody immediately before death` @desc Whether, immediately before death, the person was held in custody, and how
      `injuries in custody` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death was caused or contributed to by injuries the person sustained while held in custody
      `identity unknown` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the identity of the person who died is unknown
      `certified` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a death outside the Territory: whether the cause of death was certified by a person who is a legally qualified medical practitioner under the law of that place
      `reported` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death has been reported to a coroner (directly, or through the police)
      `investigated elsewhere` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an investigation or inquest into the death is held in an Australian State or another Territory
GIVETH A `Must the coroner investigate`
`s 14 -- must the coroner investigate` `where it happened` `what kind of death` `care` `custody` `injuries in custody` `identity unknown` `certified` `reported` `investigated elsewhere` MEANS
    IF NOT (`a reportable death` `where it happened` `what kind of death` `care` `custody` `injuries in custody` `identity unknown` `certified`)
    THEN `not required by s 14(2): not a reportable death on these facts (the text's no); a coroner has jurisdiction only if it appears to the coroner that the death is or may be a reportable death (s 14(1))`
    ELSE IF NOT `reported`
         THEN `not yet: s 14(2) binds the coroner to whom the death is reported, and it has not been reported; a person with reasonable grounds to believe it has not been reported must report it (s 12(2))`
         ELSE IF `investigated elsewhere`
              THEN `the coroner need not investigate it, because an investigation or inquest is held in a State or another Territory (s 14(3)); the coroner still may`
              ELSE `yes: the coroner to whom the death was reported must investigate it (s 14(2))`

§§ `s 15 -- Inquests`

-- s 15(1): "Where a coroner has jurisdiction to investigate a death and either the body of the deceased person is in the
-- Territory or it appears to the coroner that the death or the cause of the death occurred in the Territory, the coroner
-- must hold an inquest if: (a) the deceased was, immediately before death, a person held in care and custody; or (b) the
-- death was caused or contributed to by injuries sustained while the deceased was held in custody; or (c) the identity
-- of the deceased is not known."
-- (1A): "... the coroner may, if he or she thinks fit, hold an inquest if: (a) the body ... is in the Territory or ...
-- the death, or the cause of death, occurred in the Territory; and (b) the coroner suspects unlawful killing."
-- (2): "A coroner who has jurisdiction to investigate a death may hold an inquest as the coroner thinks fit."
-- (3): "... may hold an inquest even if: (a) the body ... is not in the Territory or is in a place from which it cannot
-- be recovered or has been destroyed; or (b) the cause of death occurred outside the Territory."
-- Finding: s 15(1)(a) says "held in care and custody", where s 12(1)(vii) says "care or custody". Encoded as written; for
-- a person held in only one of them, the answer says so and gives both readings (fork F5). Whether the coroner suspects
-- unlawful killing is the coroner's judgment: asked.
DECLARE `Must there be an inquest` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the coroner must hold an inquest (s 15(1))`
    `on the words of s 15(1)(a) ("held in care and custody") an inquest is mandatory only for a person held in both care and custody, and your relative was held in only one; if "and" is read as "or", as in s 12(1)(vii), the coroner must hold one; otherwise the coroner may hold one as he or she thinks fit (s 15(2))`
    `the coroner may hold an inquest if he or she thinks fit, because the coroner suspects unlawful killing (s 15(1A)); it is not mandatory`
    `not mandatory: the coroner may hold an inquest as he or she thinks fit (s 15(2), (3)); a decision not to hold one must be notified to the senior next of kin with reasons, and may be taken to the Supreme Court within 14 days (s 16)`
    `no inquest under this Act on these facts: not a reportable death, so on these facts the coroner has no jurisdiction to investigate it (ss 14(1), 15; the text's no)`

@export Must there be an inquest into my relative's death (Coroners Act 1993 (NT) s 15)? With a link to the Territory, an inquest is mandatory for a person held in care and custody, a death from injuries sustained in custody, or an unidentified person; the coroner may hold one where unlawful killing is suspected, and otherwise as he or she thinks fit.
GIVEN `where it happened` IS A `Where the death happened` @desc Where the death happened, and whether the person ordinarily lived in the Northern Territory
      `what kind of death` IS A `What kind of death it was` @desc Which of these best describes the death
      `care` IS A `Care immediately before death` @desc Whether, immediately before death, the person was held in care, and how
      `custody` IS A `Custody immediately before death` @desc Whether, immediately before death, the person was held in custody, and how
      `injuries in custody` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death was caused or contributed to by injuries the person sustained while held in custody
      `identity unknown` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the identity of the person who died is unknown
      `certified` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a death outside the Territory: whether the cause of death was certified by a person who is a legally qualified medical practitioner under the law of that place
      `unlawful killing suspected` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner suspects unlawful killing
GIVETH A `Must there be an inquest`
`s 15 -- must there be an inquest` `where it happened` `what kind of death` `care` `custody` `injuries in custody` `identity unknown` `certified` `unlawful killing suspected` MEANS
    IF NOT (`a reportable death` `where it happened` `what kind of death` `care` `custody` `injuries in custody` `identity unknown` `certified`)
    THEN `no inquest under this Act on these facts: not a reportable death, so on these facts the coroner has no jurisdiction to investigate it (ss 14(1), 15; the text's no)`
    ELSE IF `linked to the Territory` AND ((`held in care` `care` AND `held in custody` `custody`) OR `injuries in custody` OR `identity unknown`)
         THEN `yes: the coroner must hold an inquest (s 15(1))`
         ELSE IF `linked to the Territory` AND (`held in care` `care` OR `held in custody` `custody`)
              THEN `on the words of s 15(1)(a) ("held in care and custody") an inquest is mandatory only for a person held in both care and custody, and your relative was held in only one; if "and" is read as "or", as in s 12(1)(vii), the coroner must hold one; otherwise the coroner may hold one as he or she thinks fit (s 15(2))`
              ELSE IF `linked to the Territory` AND `unlawful killing suspected`
                   THEN `the coroner may hold an inquest if he or she thinks fit, because the coroner suspects unlawful killing (s 15(1A)); it is not mandatory`
                   ELSE `not mandatory: the coroner may hold an inquest as he or she thinks fit (s 15(2), (3)); a decision not to hold one must be notified to the senior next of kin with reasons, and may be taken to the Supreme Court within 14 days (s 16)`
    WHERE
        `linked to the Territory` MEANS `where it happened` EQUALS `in the Territory: the body is in the Territory, or the death or its cause occurred there`

§§ `Applying to the Supreme Court within the time the Act allows`

-- The family's steps with fixed deadlines are all applications to the Supreme Court: within 14 days after receiving
-- notice of a decision not to hold an inquest (s 16(2)); within 48 hours after receiving notice of a refusal to direct
-- an autopsy (s 21(3)), of a decision that an autopsy is necessary over the senior next of kin's objection (s 23(3)), or
-- of an exhumation order (s 24(4)). The Act says "may apply ... within": a permission. It is written as a MUST with a LEST
-- because missing the time is what has the consequence: the application is no longer one the section allows. Time is
-- counted in days for s 16(2) and in hours for the rest, from receipt of the notice (time 0). Fork F6.
DECLARE `Applicant` IS ONE OF `the applicant`
DECLARE `Application step` IS ONE OF `apply to the Supreme Court`

GIVEN `the time allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Applicant` `Application step`
`apply to the Supreme Court within the time allowed` `the time allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the applicant`
    MUST `apply to the Supreme Court`
    WITHIN `the time allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the applicant` BECAUSE "the time the Coroners Act allows for applying to the Supreme Court, counted from receiving the notice, has passed"

-- Runs the rule over what happened. FULFILLED means in time and anything else late. Not exported; it takes no DEONTIC
-- from outside. An application before the notice is counted as made at time 0.
GIVEN `the time allowed` IS A NUMBER, `the time` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `applying at this time is in time` `the time allowed` `the time` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`apply to the Supreme Court within the time allowed` `the time allowed`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the moment`), (EVENT `the applicant` `apply to the Supreme Court` `the moment`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
    WHERE
        `the moment` MEANS IF `the time` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `the time`

§§ `s 16 -- Decision not to hold an inquest`

-- s 16(1): "Where a coroner who has jurisdiction to hold an inquest into a death makes a decision not to hold an inquest,
-- the coroner: (a) must record the decision in writing; and (b) must specify the reasons for the decision; and (c) as
-- soon as practicable after making the decision: (i) must notify the senior next of kin of the deceased person; and (ii)
-- may notify any person; of the decision, in writing, including the reasons for the decision."
-- Fork F8: whether the asker is the senior next of kin is asked as a yes/no here, with the s 3 question beside it.
DECLARE `Must I be told of a decision not to hold an inquest` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the coroner must record the decision and its reasons in writing and, as soon as practicable, notify you of it in writing with the reasons (s 16(1)); you then have 14 days to apply to the Supreme Court (s 16(2))`
    `the coroner may notify you but need not: the duty is to notify the senior next of kin (s 16(1)(c)); the Act says no more (NOTHING-kind)`
    `s 16 does not arise: the coroner has not decided not to hold an inquest (the text's no: its "where" fails)`

@export If the coroner decides not to hold an inquest, must I be told (Coroners Act 1993 (NT) s 16(1))? The coroner must record the decision and reasons in writing and, as soon as practicable, notify the senior next of kin in writing with the reasons; anyone else may be notified.
GIVEN `I am the senior next of kin` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the senior next of kin of the person who died (see the s 3 question)
      `decided not to hold an inquest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a coroner with jurisdiction has decided not to hold an inquest into the death
GIVETH A `Must I be told of a decision not to hold an inquest`
`s 16 -- must I be told of a decision not to hold an inquest` `I am the senior next of kin` `decided not to hold an inquest` MEANS
    IF NOT `decided not to hold an inquest`
    THEN `s 16 does not arise: the coroner has not decided not to hold an inquest (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
    ELSE IF `I am the senior next of kin`
         THEN `yes: the coroner must record the decision and its reasons in writing and, as soon as practicable, notify you of it in writing with the reasons (s 16(1)); you then have 14 days to apply to the Supreme Court (s 16(2))`
         ELSE `the coroner may notify you but need not: the duty is to notify the senior next of kin (s 16(1)(c)); the Act says no more (NOTHING-kind)`

-- s 16(2): "Within 14 days after receiving notice of the decision mentioned in subsection (1)(b), the person may apply to
-- the Supreme Court for an order that an inquest be held." (3): "The Supreme Court may if it thinks fit, make an order
-- that an inquest be held."
-- Fork F7: "the person" is read as any person notified under s 16(1)(c), the senior next of kin or another; "notice of
-- the decision mentioned in subsection (1)(b)" is read as the written notice under (1)(c) (finding). The day of receipt
-- is not counted: the last day is 14 days after it.
@export What is the last day on which I can apply to the Supreme Court for an order that an inquest be held, after being told the coroner decided not to hold one (Coroners Act 1993 (NT) s 16(2))? The application must be made within 14 days after receiving the notice.
GIVEN `received on` IS A DATE @desc The day you received the coroner's written notice of the decision not to hold an inquest
GIVETH A DATE
`s 16 -- the last day to apply for an inquest` `received on` MEANS `received on` `plus days` 14

DECLARE `Can I apply to the Supreme Court for an inquest` IS ONE OF
    `in time: you applied within 14 days after receiving notice of the decision (s 16(2)); the Supreme Court may order that an inquest be held if it thinks fit (s 16(3))`
    `still in time: you have not applied, and the 14 days after receiving notice of the decision have not ended; apply by the last day (s 16(2))`
    `out of time: the 14 days after receiving notice of the decision have passed (s 16(2), the text's no); a coroner may still hold an inquest if satisfied that new facts or evidence make it necessary or desirable (s 44A(1))`
    `the 14 days have not begun: they run from receiving the written notice of the decision not to hold an inquest (s 16(2))`

@export Am I in time to apply to the Supreme Court for an order that an inquest be held into my relative's death (Coroners Act 1993 (NT) s 16(2))? A person notified of a coroner's decision not to hold an inquest may apply within 14 days after receiving the notice.
GIVEN `received notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have received the coroner's written notice of the decision not to hold an inquest
      `received on` IS A DATE @desc The day you received that notice (if you have not, any date; it is not used)
      `applied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have applied to the Supreme Court for an order that an inquest be held
      `applied on` IS A DATE @desc The day you applied (if you have not, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Can I apply to the Supreme Court for an inquest`
`s 16 -- am I in time to apply for an inquest` `received notice` `received on` `applied` `applied on` `today` MEANS
    IF NOT `received notice`
    THEN `the 14 days have not begun: they run from receiving the written notice of the decision not to hold an inquest (s 16(2))`
    ELSE IF `applied`
         THEN IF `applying at this time is in time` 14 (`the days from` `received on` `to` `applied on`)
              THEN `in time: you applied within 14 days after receiving notice of the decision (s 16(2)); the Supreme Court may order that an inquest be held if it thinks fit (s 16(3))`
              ELSE `out of time: the 14 days after receiving notice of the decision have passed (s 16(2), the text's no); a coroner may still hold an inquest if satisfied that new facts or evidence make it necessary or desirable (s 44A(1))`
         ELSE IF `applying at this time is in time` 14 (`the days from` `received on` `to` `today`)
              THEN `still in time: you have not applied, and the 14 days after receiving notice of the decision have not ended; apply by the last day (s 16(2))`
              ELSE `out of time: the 14 days after receiving notice of the decision have passed (s 16(2), the text's no); a coroner may still hold an inquest if satisfied that new facts or evidence make it necessary or desirable (s 44A(1))`

§§ `s 17 -- Control of the body`

-- s 17(1): "Where a reportable death has occurred and the body of a deceased person is in the Territory, the body is
-- under the control of the coroner investigating the death until the coroner or coroner's clerk has issued a certificate
-- permitting its disposal." (2): "Where the Supreme Court makes an order under section 16(3), a coroner or coroner's
-- clerk must not issue a certificate mentioned in subsection (1)."
-- s 21(4)(b): the Supreme Court may make an order "prohibiting disposal of the body of the deceased person until the
-- coroner has the results of the autopsy and has ordered its disposal."
-- An "until" bar (ruling (d) of 2026-11-05): the body is under the coroner's control before the certificate, and not
-- after it. Finding: s 17(2) has no end point; read literally, once the Supreme Court orders an inquest no certificate
-- can ever issue (fork F9: encoded as written, the answer says so).
DECLARE `Is the body under the coroner's control` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the body is under the coroner's control until a certificate permitting its disposal is issued (s 17(1)); and because the Supreme Court has ordered an inquest under s 16(3), the coroner or clerk must not issue that certificate (s 17(2), which on its words sets no end to the bar)`
    `yes: the body is under the coroner's control until a certificate permitting its disposal is issued (s 17(1)); and the Supreme Court's order prohibits disposal until the coroner has the autopsy results and has ordered disposal (s 21(4)(b))`
    `yes: the body is under the coroner's control until the coroner or coroner's clerk issues a certificate permitting its disposal (burial or cremation) (s 17(1))`
    `no: a certificate permitting disposal has been issued, which ends the coroner's control under s 17(1) (the text's no: "until")`
    `s 17 does not put the body under the coroner's control: not a reportable death on these facts, or the body is not in the Territory (the text's no: its "where" fails)`

@export Is my relative's body under the coroner's control, so that we cannot yet bury or cremate it (Coroners Act 1993 (NT) s 17)? Where a reportable death has occurred and the body is in the Territory, it is under the control of the coroner until the coroner or the coroner's clerk issues a certificate permitting its disposal.
GIVEN `where it happened` IS A `Where the death happened` @desc Where the death happened, and whether the person ordinarily lived in the Northern Territory
      `what kind of death` IS A `What kind of death it was` @desc Which of these best describes the death
      `care` IS A `Care immediately before death` @desc Whether, immediately before death, the person was held in care, and how
      `custody` IS A `Custody immediately before death` @desc Whether, immediately before death, the person was held in custody, and how
      `injuries in custody` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death was caused or contributed to by injuries the person sustained while held in custody
      `identity unknown` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the identity of the person who died is unknown
      `certified` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a death outside the Territory: whether the cause of death was certified by a person who is a legally qualified medical practitioner under the law of that place
      `the body is in the Territory` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the body of the person who died is in the Northern Territory
      `certificate issued` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner or the coroner's clerk has issued a certificate permitting disposal of the body
      `Supreme Court ordered an inquest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court has ordered that an inquest be held, on an application after a decision not to hold one (s 16(3))
      `Supreme Court prohibited disposal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court, on an application for an autopsy, has prohibited disposal of the body until the coroner has the autopsy results and has ordered its disposal, and that is not yet done (s 21(4)(b))
GIVETH A `Is the body under the coroner's control`
`s 17 -- is the body under the coroner's control` `where it happened` `what kind of death` `care` `custody` `injuries in custody` `identity unknown` `certified` `the body is in the Territory` `certificate issued` `Supreme Court ordered an inquest` `Supreme Court prohibited disposal` MEANS
    IF (NOT (`a reportable death` `where it happened` `what kind of death` `care` `custody` `injuries in custody` `identity unknown` `certified`)) OR (NOT `the body is in the Territory`)
    THEN `s 17 does not put the body under the coroner's control: not a reportable death on these facts, or the body is not in the Territory (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
    ELSE IF `certificate issued`
         THEN `no: a certificate permitting disposal has been issued, which ends the coroner's control under s 17(1) (the text's no: "until")`
         ELSE IF `Supreme Court ordered an inquest`
              THEN `yes: the body is under the coroner's control until a certificate permitting its disposal is issued (s 17(1)); and because the Supreme Court has ordered an inquest under s 16(3), the coroner or clerk must not issue that certificate (s 17(2), which on its words sets no end to the bar)`
              ELSE IF `Supreme Court prohibited disposal`
                   THEN `yes: the body is under the coroner's control until a certificate permitting its disposal is issued (s 17(1)); and the Supreme Court's order prohibits disposal until the coroner has the autopsy results and has ordered disposal (s 21(4)(b))`
                   ELSE `yes: the body is under the coroner's control until the coroner or coroner's clerk issues a certificate permitting its disposal (burial or cremation) (s 17(1))`

§§ `ss 21, 23 and 24 -- Autopsy and exhumation: applying to the Supreme Court within 48 hours`

DECLARE `Can I still apply within 48 hours` IS ONE OF
    `in time: you apply within 48 hours after receiving the notice, as the section allows`
    `out of time: more than 48 hours have passed since you received the notice (the text's no: a period the text fixes)`
    `the 48 hours have not begun: they run from receiving the written notice`
    `the section gives this application to the senior next of kin; it says nothing of an application by anyone else (NOTHING-kind)`
    `nothing to apply against: the coroner has not refused an autopsy that you asked for (s 21(3) runs from a refusal)`
    `nothing to apply against: no autopsy decision or exhumation order has been made`

GIVEN `received notice` IS A BOOLEAN, `hours` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `Can I still apply within 48 hours`
`the 48 hours` `received notice` `hours` MEANS
    IF NOT `received notice`
    THEN `the 48 hours have not begun: they run from receiving the written notice`
    ELSE IF `applying at this time is in time` 48 `hours`
         THEN `in time: you apply within 48 hours after receiving the notice, as the section allows`
         ELSE `out of time: more than 48 hours have passed since you received the notice (the text's no: a period the text fixes)`

-- s 21: "(1) If a coroner has jurisdiction to investigate a death, a person may ask a coroner to direct that an autopsy be
-- performed on the body of the deceased person. (2) If a coroner refuses a person's request under subsection (1), the
-- coroner must immediately give to the person notice in writing including reasons for the refusal. (3) Within 48 hours
-- after a person receives a notice of refusal mentioned in subsection (2), the person may apply to the Supreme Court for
-- an order for an autopsy. (4) The Supreme Court may, if it thinks fit, make an order: (a) directing the coroner to
-- require a medical practitioner to perform an autopsy; and (b) prohibiting disposal of the body ... until the coroner
-- has the results of the autopsy and has ordered its disposal."
@export The coroner refused my request for an autopsy: am I in time to ask the Supreme Court to order one (Coroners Act 1993 (NT) s 21(3))? Any person may ask the coroner to direct an autopsy; if the coroner refuses, it must immediately give written reasons, and the person may apply to the Supreme Court within 48 hours after receiving them.
GIVEN `I asked for an autopsy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked a coroner to direct that an autopsy be performed
      `the coroner refused` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner refused your request
      `received notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have received the coroner's written notice of the refusal, with reasons
      `hours` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours after you received that notice you applied to the Supreme Court, or, if you have not applied yet, how many hours have passed since
GIVETH A `Can I still apply within 48 hours`
`s 21 -- can I still apply to the Supreme Court for an autopsy` `I asked for an autopsy` `the coroner refused` `received notice` `hours` MEANS
    IF (NOT `I asked for an autopsy`) OR (NOT `the coroner refused`)
    THEN `nothing to apply against: the coroner has not refused an autopsy that you asked for (s 21(3) runs from a refusal)`
    ELSE `the 48 hours` `received notice` `hours`

-- s 23(3): "Within 48 hours after receiving notice of the coroner's decision under subsection (1), the senior next of kin
-- of the deceased person may apply to the Supreme Court for an order that an autopsy not be performed and the Court, in
-- its discretion, may make an order that no autopsy be performed." The application is given to the senior next of kin;
-- for anyone else the Act is silent (NOTHING-kind, a grant limited in its own wording).
@export I objected to an autopsy and the coroner decided one is necessary: am I in time to ask the Supreme Court to stop it (Coroners Act 1993 (NT) s 23(3))? The senior next of kin may apply within 48 hours after receiving notice of the coroner's decision; the Court may order that no autopsy be performed.
GIVEN `I am the senior next of kin` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the senior next of kin of the person who died (see the s 3 question)
      `the coroner decided an autopsy is necessary` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, after you asked the coroner not to direct an autopsy, the coroner decided that one is necessary
      `received notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have received the coroner's written notice of that decision
      `hours` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours after you received that notice you applied to the Supreme Court, or, if you have not applied yet, how many hours have passed since
GIVETH A `Can I still apply within 48 hours`
`s 23 -- can I still apply to the Supreme Court to stop the autopsy` `I am the senior next of kin` `the coroner decided an autopsy is necessary` `received notice` `hours` MEANS
    IF NOT `I am the senior next of kin`
    THEN `the section gives this application to the senior next of kin; it says nothing of an application by anyone else (NOTHING-kind)`
    ELSE IF NOT `the coroner decided an autopsy is necessary`
         THEN `nothing to apply against: no autopsy decision or exhumation order has been made`
         ELSE `the 48 hours` `received notice` `hours`

-- s 24(4): "Within 48 hours after receiving notice of the order under subsection (2), the senior next of kin of the
-- deceased person may apply to the Supreme Court for an order that the body of the deceased person not be exhumed and the
-- Supreme Court may, in its discretion, make an order that the body not be exhumed." The order is made under s 24(1); the
-- notice is given under s 24(2) (finding).
@export The coroner ordered that my relative's body be exhumed: am I in time to ask the Supreme Court to stop it (Coroners Act 1993 (NT) s 24(4))? The senior next of kin may apply within 48 hours after receiving notice of the exhumation order; the Court may order that the body not be exhumed.
GIVEN `I am the senior next of kin` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the senior next of kin of the person who died (see the s 3 question)
      `exhumation ordered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner has ordered that the body be exhumed
      `received notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have received written notice of the exhumation order
      `hours` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours after you received that notice you applied to the Supreme Court, or, if you have not applied yet, how many hours have passed since
GIVETH A `Can I still apply within 48 hours`
`s 24 -- can I still apply to the Supreme Court to stop the exhumation` `I am the senior next of kin` `exhumation ordered` `received notice` `hours` MEANS
    IF NOT `I am the senior next of kin`
    THEN `the section gives this application to the senior next of kin; it says nothing of an application by anyone else (NOTHING-kind)`
    ELSE IF NOT `exhumation ordered`
         THEN `nothing to apply against: no autopsy decision or exhumation order has been made`
         ELSE `the 48 hours` `received notice` `hours`

§§ `s 22 -- Notice of an autopsy on a person held in custody`

-- s 22: "Where the coroner directs a medical practitioner to perform an autopsy on a deceased person who was a person
-- held in custody, the coroner must take reasonable steps to advise the senior next of kin of the deceased person of the
-- direction." Fork F10: "was a person held in custody" is read as immediately before death (as s 12(1)(vii)).
DECLARE `Must the coroner tell me of the autopsy` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the coroner must take reasonable steps to advise you of the direction to perform an autopsy (s 22)`
    `not under s 22: the person who died was not a person held in custody (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
    `not under s 22: no autopsy has been directed (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
    `s 22 is a duty to the senior next of kin; it says nothing of advising anyone else (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Must the coroner tell me that an autopsy has been directed on my relative, who died in custody (Coroners Act 1993 (NT) s 22)? Where the coroner directs an autopsy on a person who was held in custody, the coroner must take reasonable steps to advise the senior next of kin.
GIVEN `I am the senior next of kin` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the senior next of kin of the person who died (see the s 3 question)
      `custody` IS A `Custody immediately before death` @desc Whether, immediately before death, the person was held in custody, and how
      `autopsy directed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner has directed a medical practitioner to perform an autopsy
GIVETH A `Must the coroner tell me of the autopsy`
`s 22 -- must the coroner tell me of the autopsy` `I am the senior next of kin` `custody` `autopsy directed` MEANS
    IF NOT `autopsy directed`
    THEN `not under s 22: no autopsy has been directed (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
    ELSE IF NOT (`held in custody` `custody`)
         THEN `not under s 22: the person who died was not a person held in custody (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
         ELSE IF `I am the senior next of kin`
              THEN `yes: the coroner must take reasonable steps to advise you of the direction to perform an autopsy (s 22)`
              ELSE `s 22 is a duty to the senior next of kin; it says nothing of advising anyone else (NOTHING-kind)`

§§ `s 23 -- Objection to an autopsy`

-- s 23(1): "Where the senior next of kin of the deceased person asks a coroner not to direct that an autopsy be
-- performed but the coroner decides that an autopsy is necessary, the coroner must immediately give notice in writing of
-- the decision to the senior next of kin." (2): "Unless the coroner believes that an autopsy needs to be performed
-- immediately, where a request has been made under subsection (1), an autopsy must not be performed until 48 hours after
-- the senior next of kin of the deceased person has been given notice of the coroner's decision under that subsection."
-- An "until" bar (ruling (d)): held back before the 48 hours end, not after. At exactly 48 hours the bar has lifted
-- (fork F11). The "unless" once met: the bar does not apply (fork F12). The Supreme Court's order (s 23(3)) is a flat no.
DECLARE `Can the autopsy go ahead yet` IS ONE OF
    `no: the Supreme Court has ordered that no autopsy be performed (s 23(3))`
    `not yet: the coroner must first give you written notice of the decision (s 23(1)), and the autopsy must not be performed until 48 hours after you have been given it (s 23(2))`
    `not yet: the autopsy must not be performed until 48 hours after you were given notice of the coroner's decision (s 23(2)); within those 48 hours you may apply to the Supreme Court to stop it (s 23(3))`
    `s 23(2) no longer holds it back: 48 hours have passed since you were given notice of the decision (the text's no: "until")`
    `s 23(2) does not hold it back: the coroner believes the autopsy needs to be performed immediately (s 23(2), "unless")`
    `s 23(2) does not hold it back: no request not to direct an autopsy has been made by the senior next of kin (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
    `no autopsy is in question: the coroner has not decided that an autopsy is necessary`

@export I am the senior next of kin and asked the coroner not to direct an autopsy: can the autopsy go ahead yet (Coroners Act 1993 (NT) s 23)? If the coroner decides an autopsy is necessary, it must give you written notice at once, and unless it believes the autopsy must be done immediately, the autopsy must not be performed until 48 hours after you were given that notice.
GIVEN `I am the senior next of kin` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the senior next of kin of the person who died (see the s 3 question)
      `I asked for no autopsy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked the coroner not to direct that an autopsy be performed
      `the coroner decided an autopsy is necessary` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner decided that an autopsy is necessary
      `the coroner believes it is needed immediately` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner believes that the autopsy needs to be performed immediately
      `given notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been given written notice of the coroner's decision
      `hours since notice` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours have passed since you were given that notice
      `the Supreme Court ordered no autopsy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court has ordered that no autopsy be performed
GIVETH A `Can the autopsy go ahead yet`
`s 23 -- can the autopsy go ahead yet` `I am the senior next of kin` `I asked for no autopsy` `the coroner decided an autopsy is necessary` `the coroner believes it is needed immediately` `given notice` `hours since notice` `the Supreme Court ordered no autopsy` MEANS
    IF `the Supreme Court ordered no autopsy`
    THEN `no: the Supreme Court has ordered that no autopsy be performed (s 23(3))`
    ELSE IF NOT `the coroner decided an autopsy is necessary`
         THEN `no autopsy is in question: the coroner has not decided that an autopsy is necessary`
         ELSE IF (NOT `I am the senior next of kin`) OR (NOT `I asked for no autopsy`)
              THEN `s 23(2) does not hold it back: no request not to direct an autopsy has been made by the senior next of kin (the text's no: its "where" fails)`
              ELSE IF `the coroner believes it is needed immediately`
                   THEN `s 23(2) does not hold it back: the coroner believes the autopsy needs to be performed immediately (s 23(2), "unless")`
                   ELSE IF NOT `given notice`
                        THEN `not yet: the coroner must first give you written notice of the decision (s 23(1)), and the autopsy must not be performed until 48 hours after you have been given it (s 23(2))`
                        ELSE IF `hours since notice` LESS THAN 48
                             THEN `not yet: the autopsy must not be performed until 48 hours after you were given notice of the coroner's decision (s 23(2)); within those 48 hours you may apply to the Supreme Court to stop it (s 23(3))`
                             ELSE `s 23(2) no longer holds it back: 48 hours have passed since you were given notice of the decision (the text's no: "until")`

§§ `s 24 -- Exhumation`

-- s 24(1): "The coroner may order that the body of a deceased person be exhumed if the coroner reasonably believes that it
-- is necessary for an investigation of a death." (2): "The coroner must ensure that at least 48 hours notice in writing is
-- given to the senior next of kin of the deceased person and to [the cemetery's responsible entity, representative or
-- interest holders] before the body is exhumed unless the coroner is satisfied it is not possible to give the notice."
-- (3): "If the senior next of kin of the deceased person asks the coroner not to exhume the body of the deceased person,
-- the body must not be exhumed until 48 hours after the request has been made."
-- Two "until" bars (ruling (d)). The notice to the cemetery is not the family's: not asked. Fork F11 for the 48th hour;
-- fork F12 for the "unless" once met.
DECLARE `Can the body be exhumed yet` IS ONE OF
    `no: the Supreme Court has ordered that the body not be exhumed (s 24(4))`
    `not yet: at least 48 hours' written notice must be given to you before the body is exhumed (s 24(2)); within 48 hours after receiving it you may apply to the Supreme Court to stop it (s 24(4))`
    `not yet: you asked the coroner not to exhume the body, and it must not be exhumed until 48 hours after your request (s 24(3))`
    `s 24 no longer holds it back: the notice has run its 48 hours (or the coroner was satisfied it could not be given), and any request of yours not to exhume was made 48 hours ago or more`
    `no exhumation is in question: the coroner has not ordered one`
    `s 24's notice and request are the senior next of kin's; the section says nothing of anyone else (NOTHING-kind)`

@export I am the senior next of kin: can my relative's body be exhumed yet (Coroners Act 1993 (NT) s 24)? The coroner may order exhumation; at least 48 hours' written notice must be given to the senior next of kin unless the coroner is satisfied that is not possible, and if the senior next of kin asks the coroner not to exhume, the body must not be exhumed until 48 hours after the request.
GIVEN `I am the senior next of kin` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the senior next of kin of the person who died (see the s 3 question)
      `exhumation ordered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner has ordered that the body be exhumed
      `the coroner is satisfied notice is not possible` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner is satisfied that it is not possible to give you notice
      `given notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been given written notice of the exhumation
      `hours since notice` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours have passed since you were given that notice
      `I asked for no exhumation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked the coroner not to exhume the body
      `hours since my request` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours have passed since you made that request
      `the Supreme Court ordered no exhumation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court has ordered that the body not be exhumed
GIVETH A `Can the body be exhumed yet`
`s 24 -- can the body be exhumed yet` `I am the senior next of kin` `exhumation ordered` `the coroner is satisfied notice is not possible` `given notice` `hours since notice` `I asked for no exhumation` `hours since my request` `the Supreme Court ordered no exhumation` MEANS
    IF NOT `I am the senior next of kin`
    THEN `s 24's notice and request are the senior next of kin's; the section says nothing of anyone else (NOTHING-kind)`
    ELSE IF NOT `exhumation ordered`
         THEN `no exhumation is in question: the coroner has not ordered one`
         ELSE IF `the Supreme Court ordered no exhumation`
              THEN `no: the Supreme Court has ordered that the body not be exhumed (s 24(4))`
              ELSE IF (NOT `the coroner is satisfied notice is not possible`) AND ((NOT `given notice`) OR `hours since notice` LESS THAN 48)
                   THEN `not yet: at least 48 hours' written notice must be given to you before the body is exhumed (s 24(2)); within 48 hours after receiving it you may apply to the Supreme Court to stop it (s 24(4))`
                   ELSE IF `I asked for no exhumation` AND `hours since my request` LESS THAN 48
                        THEN `not yet: you asked the coroner not to exhume the body, and it must not be exhumed until 48 hours after your request (s 24(3))`
                        ELSE `s 24 no longer holds it back: the notice has run its 48 hours (or the coroner was satisfied it could not be given), and any request of yours not to exhume was made 48 hours ago or more`

§§ `The inquest -- s 37 Advertisement`

-- s 37: "A coroner's clerk must, not less than 14 days before an inquest, publish in a newspaper circulating generally in
-- the area, the date, time, place and subject of the inquest." Fork F13: counted by plain subtraction (published on the
-- 1st for an inquest on the 15th is 14 days before); the other reading needs 14 clear days.
@export What is the latest day the coroner's clerk must advertise the inquest into my relative's death in a newspaper (Coroners Act 1993 (NT) s 37)? The date, time, place and subject of the inquest must be published not less than 14 days before it.
GIVEN `inquest day` IS A DATE @desc The day the inquest is to be held
GIVETH A DATE
`s 37 -- the latest day to advertise the inquest` `inquest day` MEANS `inquest day` `plus days` (0 MINUS 14)

DECLARE `Was the inquest advertised in time` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the inquest was advertised not less than 14 days before it (s 37)`
    `no: the inquest was advertised less than 14 days before it (s 37, the text's no)`
    `not yet advertised, and there is still time: the clerk must publish it by the latest day (s 37)`
    `not yet advertised, and the latest day has passed: it can no longer be advertised not less than 14 days before the inquest (s 37, the text's no)`

@export Was the inquest into my relative's death advertised early enough (Coroners Act 1993 (NT) s 37)? The coroner's clerk must publish its date, time, place and subject in a newspaper circulating generally in the area not less than 14 days before the inquest.
GIVEN `inquest day` IS A DATE @desc The day the inquest is to be held
      `advertised` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the inquest has been advertised in a newspaper circulating generally in the area
      `advertised on` IS A DATE @desc The day it was advertised (if it has not been, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Was the inquest advertised in time`
`s 37 -- was the inquest advertised in time` `inquest day` `advertised` `advertised on` `today` MEANS
    IF `advertised`
    THEN IF `the latest day` `falls before` `advertised on`
         THEN `no: the inquest was advertised less than 14 days before it (s 37, the text's no)`
         ELSE `yes: the inquest was advertised not less than 14 days before it (s 37)`
    ELSE IF `the latest day` `falls before` `today`
         THEN `not yet advertised, and the latest day has passed: it can no longer be advertised not less than 14 days before the inquest (s 37, the text's no)`
         ELSE `not yet advertised, and there is still time: the clerk must publish it by the latest day (s 37)`
    WHERE
        `the latest day` MEANS `s 37 -- the latest day to advertise the inquest` `inquest day`

§§ `The inquest -- s 40 Rights of interested persons`

-- s 40(3): "A person who, in the opinion of the coroner, has a sufficient interest may, at an inquest, appear or be
-- represented, call and examine or cross-examine witnesses, and make submissions." (2): the coroner may make available a
-- statement it intends to consider to such a person. A grant limited in its own wording: without the coroner's opinion,
-- the Act says no more (NOTHING-kind).
DECLARE `May I take part in the inquest` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may appear or be represented, call and examine or cross-examine witnesses, and make submissions (s 40(3)); the coroner may also make available to you a statement it intends to consider (s 40(2))`
    `s 40(3) gives these rights to a person the coroner considers has a sufficient interest; the coroner has not formed that opinion of you, and the Act says no more (NOTHING-kind)`

@export May I appear at the inquest, be represented, question witnesses and make submissions (Coroners Act 1993 (NT) s 40(3))? A person who, in the coroner's opinion, has a sufficient interest may do all of these.
GIVEN `the coroner considers I have a sufficient interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner considers that you have a sufficient interest in the inquest
GIVETH A `May I take part in the inquest`
`s 40 -- may I take part in the inquest` `the coroner considers I have a sufficient interest` MEANS
    IF `the coroner considers I have a sufficient interest`
    THEN `yes: you may appear or be represented, call and examine or cross-examine witnesses, and make submissions (s 40(3)); the coroner may also make available to you a statement it intends to consider (s 40(2))`
    ELSE `s 40(3) gives these rights to a person the coroner considers has a sufficient interest; the coroner has not formed that opinion of you, and the Act says no more (NOTHING-kind)`

§§ `The inquest -- s 38 Witnesses and self-incrimination`

-- s 38(2): "A person who has been offered a certificate under subsection (1) is no longer entitled to refuse to answer
-- questions on the ground that his or her answers will criminate or tend to criminate him or her ..." (3): "Where a
-- person is given a certificate under this section in respect of evidence given at an inquest, a statement by the person
-- as part of that evidence in answer to a question is not admissible in evidence in criminal or civil proceedings, or in
-- proceedings before a tribunal or person exercising powers and functions in a judicial manner, against the person other
-- than on a prosecution for perjury."
-- Fork F14: "other than on a prosecution for perjury" leaves that case unanswered by s 38 (NOTHING-kind), not admitted.
DECLARE `Can I refuse to answer` IS ONE OF
    `no: once the coroner has offered you a certificate, you are no longer entitled to refuse on the ground that your answer may incriminate you (s 38(2)); when you have given evidence the coroner must give you the certificate`
    `the coroner has not offered you a certificate under s 38(1); s 38 does not take away a refusal on the ground of self-incrimination, and the Act says no more (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Can I refuse to answer a question at the inquest because the answer may incriminate me (Coroners Act 1993 (NT) s 38(2))? Once the coroner offers a witness a certificate under s 38, the witness can no longer refuse on that ground.
GIVEN `offered a certificate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner has told you that, if you answer, you will be given a certificate under s 38
GIVETH A `Can I refuse to answer`
`s 38 -- can I refuse to answer` `offered a certificate` MEANS
    IF `offered a certificate`
    THEN `no: once the coroner has offered you a certificate, you are no longer entitled to refuse on the ground that your answer may incriminate you (s 38(2)); when you have given evidence the coroner must give you the certificate`
    ELSE `the coroner has not offered you a certificate under s 38(1); s 38 does not take away a refusal on the ground of self-incrimination, and the Act says no more (NOTHING-kind)`

DECLARE `Later proceedings` IS ONE OF
    `criminal proceedings against me (other than for perjury)`
    `civil proceedings against me`
    `proceedings against me before a tribunal or a person acting judicially`
    `a prosecution of me for perjury`
    `proceedings against someone else`

DECLARE `Can my answers be used against me` IS ONE OF
    `no: with a certificate under s 38, a statement you made in answer to a question as part of that evidence is not admissible against you in these proceedings (s 38(3))`
    `s 38(3) does not protect it on a prosecution for perjury ("other than"); the Act says no more (NOTHING-kind)`
    `s 38(3) protects you only in proceedings against you; for proceedings against someone else the Act says no more (NOTHING-kind)`
    `without a certificate under s 38, the Act gives your answers no protection and says no more (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Can what I said at the inquest be used against me in later criminal, civil or tribunal proceedings (Coroners Act 1993 (NT) s 38(3))? If the coroner gave you a certificate under s 38, your answers are not admissible against you, except on a prosecution for perjury.
GIVEN `given a certificate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner gave you a certificate under s 38 for the evidence you gave at the inquest
      `the proceedings` IS A `Later proceedings` @desc The later proceedings in which your answers might be used
GIVETH A `Can my answers be used against me`
`s 38 -- can my answers be used against me` `given a certificate` `the proceedings` MEANS
    IF NOT `given a certificate`
    THEN `without a certificate under s 38, the Act gives your answers no protection and says no more (NOTHING-kind)`
    ELSE IF `the proceedings` EQUALS `a prosecution of me for perjury`
         THEN `s 38(3) does not protect it on a prosecution for perjury ("other than"); the Act says no more (NOTHING-kind)`
         ELSE IF `the proceedings` EQUALS `proceedings against someone else`
              THEN `s 38(3) protects you only in proceedings against you; for proceedings against someone else the Act says no more (NOTHING-kind)`
              ELSE `no: with a certificate under s 38, a statement you made in answer to a question as part of that evidence is not admissible against you in these proceedings (s 38(3))`

§§ `The inquest -- s 43 Non-publication of the deceased's name`

-- s 43(1): "A coroner must order that a report of an inquest or of part of the proceedings, or of evidence given at an
-- inquest, not be published if the coroner reasonably believes that, to publish the report, would: ... (c) involve the
-- disclosure of details of sensitive personal matters including, where the senior next of kin of the deceased have so
-- requested, the name of the deceased." (2): breach of the order: maximum 85 penalty units or imprisonment for 2 years.
-- The coroner's belief is asked. Without a request, "including" leaves the list open: the Act says no more of the name
-- (NOTHING-kind).
DECLARE `Must the coroner suppress the name` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the senior next of kin's request makes the name a sensitive personal matter, and since the coroner believes publishing the report would disclose it, the coroner must order that the report not be published (s 43(1)(c)); publishing in breach carries up to 85 penalty units or 2 years' imprisonment (s 43(2))`
    `no order is required by s 43(1)(c): the coroner does not reasonably believe that publishing the report would disclose the name (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
    `the senior next of kin has not asked; s 43(1)(c) then does not make the name itself a sensitive personal matter, and the Act says no more about it (NOTHING-kind); the other grounds in s 43(1) may still apply`

@export Must the coroner order that a report of the inquest naming my relative not be published (Coroners Act 1993 (NT) s 43(1)(c))? Where the senior next of kin asks, the name of the person who died is a sensitive personal matter, and the coroner must order non-publication of a report it reasonably believes would disclose it.
GIVEN `the senior next of kin asked` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the senior next of kin asked that the name of the person who died not be published
      `the coroner believes the report would disclose the name` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner reasonably believes that publishing the report would disclose the name
GIVETH A `Must the coroner suppress the name`
`s 43 -- must the coroner suppress the name` `the senior next of kin asked` `the coroner believes the report would disclose the name` MEANS
    IF NOT `the senior next of kin asked`
    THEN `the senior next of kin has not asked; s 43(1)(c) then does not make the name itself a sensitive personal matter, and the Act says no more about it (NOTHING-kind); the other grounds in s 43(1) may still apply`
    ELSE IF `the coroner believes the report would disclose the name`
         THEN `yes: the senior next of kin's request makes the name a sensitive personal matter, and since the coroner believes publishing the report would disclose it, the coroner must order that the report not be published (s 43(1)(c)); publishing in breach carries up to 85 penalty units or 2 years' imprisonment (s 43(2))`
         ELSE `no order is required by s 43(1)(c): the coroner does not reasonably believe that publishing the report would disclose the name (the text's no: its "if" fails)`

§§ `After the inquest -- s 44 New inquest`

-- s 44(1): "A person may apply to the Supreme Court for an order that some or all the findings of an inquest are void."
-- (2): the Court "may declare that some or all the findings ... are void and may order a coroner: (a) to hold a new
-- inquest ...; or (b) to re-open ... an inquest and to re-examine a finding." (3): "The Supreme Court may make an order
-- under subsection (2) if it is satisfied that: (a) it is necessary because of fraud, consideration of evidence, failure
-- to consider evidence, irregularity of proceedings or insufficiency of inquiry; or (b) there is a mistake in the record
-- of the findings; or (c) there are new facts or evidence; or (d) the findings are against the evidence and the weight of
-- evidence." No time limit is set. Whether the Court is satisfied is the Court's: the person states the grounds relied on.
-- s 44A(1): after a decision not to hold an inquest, a coroner may hold one if satisfied new facts or evidence make it
-- necessary or desirable (the coroner's own power; the family has no application under it).
DECLARE `Can I ask for a new inquest` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may apply to the Supreme Court (s 44(1)), and you rely on a ground on which, if satisfied, the Court may declare findings void and order a new or re-opened inquest (s 44(2), (3)); the Act sets no time limit`
    `you may apply (s 44(1)), but the Court may make the order only if satisfied of a ground in s 44(3), and you rely on none (the text's no: its "if" fails)`
    `s 44 is about the findings of an inquest, and none has been held; against a decision not to hold one see s 16(2) (14 days), and a coroner may hold one on new facts or evidence (s 44A(1))`

@export Can I ask the Supreme Court to set aside the inquest findings and order a new or re-opened inquest (Coroners Act 1993 (NT) s 44)? Any person may apply; the Court may do so if satisfied of fraud, failure or irregularity in considering evidence or in the proceedings, insufficient inquiry, a mistake in the record, new facts or evidence, or findings against the weight of evidence.
GIVEN `an inquest was held` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an inquest into the death has been held and findings made
      `fraud or irregularity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you say a new inquest is necessary because of fraud, the consideration of evidence, a failure to consider evidence, irregularity of proceedings or insufficiency of inquiry
      `a mistake in the record` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you say there is a mistake in the record of the findings
      `new facts or evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you say there are new facts or evidence
      `against the weight of evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you say the findings are against the evidence and the weight of evidence
GIVETH A `Can I ask for a new inquest`
`s 44 -- can I ask for a new inquest` `an inquest was held` `fraud or irregularity` `a mistake in the record` `new facts or evidence` `against the weight of evidence` MEANS
    IF NOT `an inquest was held`
    THEN `s 44 is about the findings of an inquest, and none has been held; against a decision not to hold one see s 16(2) (14 days), and a coroner may hold one on new facts or evidence (s 44A(1))`
    ELSE IF `fraud or irregularity` OR `a mistake in the record` OR `new facts or evidence` OR `against the weight of evidence`
         THEN `yes: you may apply to the Supreme Court (s 44(1)), and you rely on a ground on which, if satisfied, the Court may declare findings void and order a new or re-opened inquest (s 44(2), (3)); the Act sets no time limit`
         ELSE `you may apply (s 44(1)), but the Court may make the order only if satisfied of a ground in s 44(3), and you rely on none (the text's no: its "if" fails)`

§§ `Using the coronial record in a claim -- ss 11(2) and 45`

-- s 11(1): the coroner or clerk "must keep a record of findings, evidence and comments in relation to each
-- investigation". (2): "A record mentioned in subsection (1) is not admissible as evidence of a matter contained in it."
-- A flat negative: the text's no. For another use the Act says no more (NOTHING-kind).
DECLARE `Use of the record` IS ONE OF
    `as evidence of a matter it records (for example, the cause of death or how the death happened)`
    `for another purpose (for example, to show that the coroner made a finding, or what was said)`

DECLARE `Can I use the coroner's record` IS ONE OF
    `no: the coroner's record of findings, evidence and comments is not admissible as evidence of a matter contained in it (s 11(2), the text's no)`
    `s 11(2) bars only using the record as evidence of a matter contained in it; for another use the Act says no more (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Can I use the coroner's record of findings, evidence and comments as evidence in my claim (Coroners Act 1993 (NT) s 11(2))? The record is not admissible as evidence of a matter contained in it.
GIVEN `the use` IS A `Use of the record` @desc What you want to use the coroner's record for
GIVETH A `Can I use the coroner's record`
`s 11 -- can I use the coroner's record` `the use` MEANS
    IF `the use` EQUALS `as evidence of a matter it records (for example, the cause of death or how the death happened)`
    THEN `no: the coroner's record of findings, evidence and comments is not admissible as evidence of a matter contained in it (s 11(2), the text's no)`
    ELSE `s 11(2) bars only using the record as evidence of a matter contained in it; for another use the Act says no more (NOTHING-kind)`

-- s 45: "(1) A coroner must not be called to give evidence in a court or judicial proceedings about anything coming to
-- his or her knowledge in carrying out a coroner's powers, duties or functions under this Act. (2) Subsection (1) does
-- not apply in relation to proceedings against a coroner for an offence." A flat negative; s 45(2) lifts it, and the Act
-- says no more then (NOTHING-kind).
DECLARE `Can I call the coroner as a witness` IS ONE OF
    `no: a coroner must not be called to give evidence in a court or judicial proceedings about anything learned as coroner (s 45(1), the text's no)`
    `s 45(1) does not apply to proceedings against a coroner for an offence (s 45(2)); the Act says no more (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Can I call the coroner as a witness in my court case about what the coroner learned in the investigation (Coroners Act 1993 (NT) s 45)? A coroner must not be called to give evidence about anything learned as coroner, except in proceedings against the coroner for an offence.
GIVEN `proceedings against the coroner for an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceedings are proceedings against the coroner for an offence
GIVETH A `Can I call the coroner as a witness`
`s 45 -- can I call the coroner as a witness` `proceedings against the coroner for an offence` MEANS
    IF `proceedings against the coroner for an offence`
    THEN `s 45(1) does not apply to proceedings against a coroner for an offence (s 45(2)); the Act says no more (NOTHING-kind)`
    ELSE `no: a coroner must not be called to give evidence in a court or judicial proceedings about anything learned as coroner (s 45(1), the text's no)`

§§ `Offences -- maximum penalties`

-- The offences a member of the public can commit under the Act, with the maximum penalty stated under each. Penalty
-- units are given as units (their dollar value is fixed under the Penalty Units Act 2009 (NT), not deposited). Offences
-- only a medical practitioner (s 12(3)), a custodian (s 12(5)) or a police officer (s 25(2)) can commit are out (not the
-- family's). s 46 reads "Maximum penalty: 40 penalty or imprisonment for 6 months": read as 40 penalty units (fork F15).
-- s 41(3) reads "40 penalty units. or imprisonment for 6 months": read as 40 penalty units or 6 months (finding).
DECLARE `Offence` IS ONE OF
    `not reporting a reportable death you have reasonable grounds to believe has not been reported (s 12(2))`
    `not giving the coroner information that may help, after reporting a death (s 13(1))`
    `not complying with an undertaking given when a thing taken by the coroner was released (s 19(4) or s 33(4))`
    `entering or interfering with a restricted disaster area without good reason (s 32(2))`
    `not complying with a coroner's direction to give information, without reasonable excuse (s 36(2))`
    `disobeying a coroner's summons, order or direction at an inquest (s 41(3))`
    `publishing a report in breach of a non-publication order (s 43(2))`
    `insulting a coroner, interrupting an inquest, or causing a disturbance in or near the place of an inquest (s 46)`

@export What is the most I can be fined, in penalty units, for an offence under the Coroners Act 1993 (NT)? Most offences carry up to 40 penalty units; publishing in breach of a non-publication order carries up to 85.
GIVEN `the offence` IS AN `Offence` @desc The offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum fine in penalty units` `the offence` MEANS
    IF `the offence` EQUALS `publishing a report in breach of a non-publication order (s 43(2))`
    THEN 85
    ELSE 40

@export What is the longest I can be imprisoned, in months, for an offence under the Coroners Act 1993 (NT)? 0 means the offence carries no imprisonment. Entering a restricted disaster area, disobeying a coroner at an inquest and contempt carry up to 6 months; publishing in breach of a non-publication order up to 2 years.
GIVEN `the offence` IS AN `Offence` @desc The offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum imprisonment in months` `the offence` MEANS
    IF `the offence` EQUALS `publishing a report in breach of a non-publication order (s 43(2))`
    THEN 24
    ELSE IF `the offence` EQUALS `entering or interfering with a restricted disaster area without good reason (s 32(2))`
              OR `the offence` EQUALS `disobeying a coroner's summons, order or direction at an inquest (s 41(3))`
              OR `the offence` EQUALS `insulting a coroner, interrupting an inquest, or causing a disturbance in or near the place of an inquest (s 46)`
         THEN 6
         ELSE 0
